<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chapter-complex"><title>Complex Numbers</title>
              <section xml:id="complex-intro"><title>Beyond the real numbers</title>
                <p>We define <m>i</m> by <m>i^2=-1</m>. A complex number has the form <m>a+bi</m>, where <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are real.</p>
                <p>On the Argand diagram, <m>a+bi</m> is represented by the point <m>(a,b)</m>. The horizontal axis shows the real part and the vertical axis shows the imaginary part.</p>
                <p>Multiplication by <m>i</m> sends <m>a+bi</m> to <m>-b+ai</m>: a quarter-turn anticlockwise about the origin. Division by <m>i</m> produces a quarter-turn clockwise.</p>
                <p>For classroom exploration and step-by-step calculations, <url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/apps/apps/complex-numbers/operations.html" visual="open the standalone Complex Number Calculations app full screen"/>.</p>
              </section>
              
<section xml:id="complex-notation"><title>Reading complex-number notation</title>
<p>The bar above a complex number denotes its conjugate. The vertical bars on either side denote its modulus. These are different operations.</p>
<example><title>Read the symbols directly</title><p><me>\overline{2+3i}=2-3i</me><me>|2+3i|=\sqrt{2^2+3^2}=\sqrt{13}</me></p></example>
<example><title>Use named complex numbers</title><p>Let <m>z_1=2+3i</m>, <m>z_2=3-4i</m> and <m>z_3=-2+5i</m>.</p><p><me>\overline{z_1}=2-3i,\qquad \overline{z_2}=3+4i,\qquad \overline{z_3}=-2-5i.</me><me>|z_1|=\sqrt{13},\qquad |z_2|=5,\qquad |z_3|=\sqrt{29}.</me></p><p>Squaring the modulus gives <m>|z_2|^2=25</m>, not <m>5</m>. Conjugating a real number leaves it unchanged; the modulus of a negative real number is positive.</p></example>
<exercise><statement><p>Let <m>z_1=-4+3i</m> and <m>z_2=-6i</m>. Find <m>\overline{z_1}</m>, <m>|z_1|</m>, <m>\overline{z_2}</m> and <m>|z_2|</m>.</p></statement><answer><p><m>-4-3i,\ 5,\ 6i,\ 6</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
</section>
<section xml:id="complex-powers-i"><title>Powers of the imaginary unit</title>
<p>Multiplying by <m>i</m> repeatedly produces a cycle of length four. On the Argand diagram each multiplication is an anticlockwise quarter-turn.</p>
<p><me>i^0=1,\qquad i^1=i,\qquad i^2=-1,\qquad i^3=-i,\qquad i^4=1.</me></p>
<p>Since <m>i^4=1</m>, write a non-negative integer exponent as <m>n=4q+r</m>, where <m>r</m> is the remainder on division by four, one of <m>0,1,2,3</m>.</p>
<p><me>i^n=(i^4)^qi^r=i^r.</me></p>
<example><title>Reduce the exponent modulo four</title><p><md><mrow>38&amp;=4(9)+2</mrow><mrow>i^{38}&amp;=(i^4)^9i^2=-1.</mrow></md></p><p>Similarly, <m>103=4(25)+3</m>, so <m>i^{103}=-i</m>.</p></example>
<example><title>Combine the index laws with the cycle</title><p><me>i^5i^7=i^{12}=1</me><me>\dfrac{i^{19}}{i^7}=i^{12}=1.</me></p></example>
<exercise><statement><p>Simplify <m>i^{17}</m>, <m>i^{52}</m>, <m>i^{63}</m> and <m>i^{2026}</m>.</p></statement><answer><p><m>i,\ 1,\ -i,\ -1</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
</section>
<section xml:id="complex-imaginary-products"><title>Multiplying purely imaginary terms</title>
<p>Multiply the real coefficients, then replace <m>i^2</m> by <m>-1</m>. Thus <m>(ai)(bi)=-ab</m>.</p>
<example><title>Four sign combinations</title><p><md><mrow>(3i)(4i)&amp;=12i^2=-12</mrow><mrow>(-3i)(-4i)&amp;=12i^2=-12</mrow><mrow>(3i)(-4i)&amp;=-12i^2=12</mrow><mrow>(-3i)(4i)&amp;=-12i^2=12.</mrow></md></p></example>
<p><em>Same-sign non-zero imaginary terms give a negative real product.</em> Positive times positive is negative; negative times negative is negative. <em>Opposite-sign non-zero imaginary terms give a positive real product.</em> Positive times negative and negative times positive are both positive. A zero factor gives zero.</p>
<p>Do not confuse these rules with the ordinary multiplication of real numbers. The extra minus sign comes from <m>i^2=-1</m>.</p>
<exercise><statement><p>Calculate <m>(5i)(2i)</m>, <m>(-5i)(-2i)</m>, <m>(5i)(-2i)</m> and <m>(-5i)(2i)</m>.</p></statement><answer><p><m>-10,-10,10,10</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
</section>
<section xml:id="complex-scaling"><title>Multiplying by a real number</title>
<p>Distribute the real coefficient to both parts of a complex number: <m>k(a+bi)=ka+kbi</m>.</p>
<example><title>Positive and negative coefficients</title><p><me>3(2-5i)=6-15i</me><me>-2(3+4i)=-6-8i.</me></p></example>
<p>Multiplying by a negative real number also reverses both coordinates on the Argand diagram.</p>
<exercise><statement><p>Expand <m>-3(2-4i)</m> and <m>4(-2+3i)</m>.</p></statement><answer><p><m>-6+12i</m> and <m>-8+12i</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
</section>
<section xml:id="complex-add-subtract"><title>Addition and subtraction</title>
<p>Combine the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.</p>
<example><title>Adding and subtracting</title><p><me>(2+5i)+(2+3i)=4+8i</me><me>(2+5i)-(2+3i)=2i</me></p></example>
<example><title>Coefficients on both complex numbers</title><p><md><mrow>2(2+5i)-3(2+3i)&amp;=4+10i-6-9i</mrow><mrow>&amp;=-2+i.</mrow></md></p><p>Let <m>z_1=2+3i</m> and <m>z_2=3-4i</m>.</p><p><md><mrow>2z_1+3z_2&amp;=2(2+3i)+3(3-4i)</mrow><mrow>&amp;=4+6i+9-12i=13-6i.</mrow></md></p></example>
<exercise><statement><p>Using these values of <m>z_1</m> and <m>z_2</m>, calculate <m>3z_1-2z_2</m>.</p></statement><answer><p><m>17i</m>.</p></answer></exercise><p><url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/apps/apps/complex-numbers/operations.html#add" visual="Addition app"/> · <url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/apps/apps/complex-numbers/operations.html#subtract" visual="Subtraction app"/> · <url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/apps/apps/complex-numbers/operations.html#scalar" visual="Multiplication by a real number app"/>.</p>
</section>
<section xml:id="complex-multiply"><title>Multiplication</title>
<p>Expand the brackets using the distributive law, substitute <m>i^2=-1</m>, then collect like terms.</p>
<example><title>Multiply two complex numbers</title><p><md><mrow>(2+5i)(2+3i)&amp;=4+6i+10i+15i^2</mrow><mrow>&amp;=4-15+16i</mrow><mrow>&amp;=-11+16i</mrow></md></p></example>
<p><url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/apps/apps/complex-numbers/operations.html#multiply" visual="Multiplication app"/>.</p>
<exercise><statement><p>Calculate <m>(3+2i)(4-i)</m>.</p></statement><answer><p><m>14+5i</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
</section>
<section xml:id="complex-conjugate-modulus"><title>Conjugate and modulus</title>
<definition><statement><p>If <m>z=a+bi</m>, its complex conjugate is <m>\overline z=a-bi</m>. Its modulus is <m>|z|=\sqrt{a^2+b^2}</m>, the distance from the origin on an Argand diagram.</p></statement></definition>
<p>Multiplying a complex number by its conjugate eliminates the imaginary terms:</p>
<p><md><mrow>z\overline z&amp;=(a+bi)(a-bi)</mrow><mrow>&amp;=a^2-b^2i^2</mrow><mrow>&amp;=a^2+b^2=|z|^2.</mrow></md></p>
<example><title>Conjugate and modulus of <m>2+3i</m></title><p><me>\overline z=2-3i,\qquad |z|=\sqrt{13},\qquad z\overline z=13.</me></p></example>
<p><url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/apps/apps/complex-numbers/operations.html#conjugate" visual="Conjugate app"/> · <url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/apps/apps/complex-numbers/operations.html#modulus" visual="Modulus app"/> · <url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/apps/apps/complex-numbers/operations.html#plot" visual="Argand diagram app"/>.</p>
</section>
<section xml:id="complex-divide"><title>Division</title>
<p>How do we divide by a complex number? Multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. This is multiplication by one, and it makes the denominator real.</p>
<p>For <m>z_1=a+bi</m> and <m>z_2=c+di\ne0</m>,</p>
<p><md><mrow>\dfrac{z_1}{z_2}&amp;=\dfrac{z_1}{z_2}\dfrac{\overline{z_2}}{\overline{z_2}}</mrow><mrow>&amp;=\dfrac{z_1\overline{z_2}}{|z_2|^2}</mrow><mrow>&amp;=\dfrac{(a+bi)(c-di)}{c^2+d^2}.</mrow></md></p>
<p>The denominator is the <em>square</em> of the modulus, not the modulus. Do not memorise a long formula: conjugate, multiply and simplify.</p>
<example xml:id="complex-divide-worked"><title>Divide <m>2+5i</m> by <m>2+3i</m></title>
<p><md><mrow>\dfrac{2+5i}{2+3i}&amp;=\dfrac{(2+5i)(2-3i)}{(2+3i)(2-3i)}</mrow><mrow>&amp;=\dfrac{(2+5i)(2-3i)}{2^2+3^2}</mrow><mrow>&amp;=\dfrac{4-6i+10i-15i^2}{13}</mrow><mrow>&amp;=\dfrac{19+4i}{13}</mrow><mrow>&amp;=\dfrac{19}{13}+\dfrac{4}{13}i.</mrow></md></p>
<p>Check by multiplying the answer by <m>2+3i</m>. The result must be <m>2+5i</m>.</p></example>
<p><url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/apps/apps/complex-numbers/operations.html#divide" visual="Open the dedicated division app"/>. Change the coefficients and reveal one step at a time.</p>
<exercise xml:id="complex-divide-checkpoint"><statement><p>Express <m>\dfrac{3+4i}{1-2i}</m> in the form <m>a+bi</m>.</p></statement><answer><p><m>-1+2i</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
<p>For further practice, complete the class worksheet before checking the answers.</p>
</section>
<section xml:id="complex-reading"><title>Further reading on complex numbers</title>
                <p>These are external references for further explanation and study. The IB course notes are independent of the Irish Leaving Certificate syllabus; the PreTeXt books range from introductory material to undergraduate complex analysis.</p>
                <p><url href="https://aa.selfsg.com/units/u19-complex-numbers/" visual="IB Mathematics AA: Complex Numbers"/> covers Cartesian form, conjugates, the Argand diagram, modulus, argument, powers and geometric interpretation.</p>
                <p><url href="https://complexanalysis.org/web/ch_Complex_Numbers.html" visual="Howell and Mathews: Complex Analysis, Chapter 1"/> develops the algebra and geometry of complex numbers.</p>
                <p><url href="https://judsonbooks.org/aata-files/aata-html/cyclic-section-mult-group-c.html" visual="Judson: Multiplicative Group of Complex Numbers"/> covers complex operations, modulus, argument and the roots of unity.</p>
                <p><url href="https://www.cs.uleth.ca/~morris/Combinatorics/Combinatorics.html" visual="Morris: Combinatorics, Appendix A: Complex Numbers"/> provides an introductory account in a PreTeXt textbook.</p>
                <p><url href="https://matthbeck.github.io/papers/complex.pdf" visual="Beck et al.: A First Course in Complex Analysis (PDF, PreTeXt edition)"/> introduces complex-number arithmetic and geometry.</p>
                <p><url href="https://pretextbook.org/examples/sample-book/structural/html/section-cyclic-complex-numbers.html" visual="PreTeXt Sample Book: Cyclic Groups of Complex Numbers"/> also demonstrates how complex-number mathematics can be presented in PreTeXt.</p>
              </section>
            </chapter>
